    "segments": [
      {
        "role": "lecturer",
        "speaker": "LECTURER",
        "sourceStart": 10,
        "sourceEnd": 777,
        "text": "Good morning. Today we look at a small bridge project. It is a simulated case, not a real place. The town of Riverton wanted a new footbridge over the river. Two plans were discussed. Plan A was cheaper. Plan B was stronger. The council chose Plan B. Why? Because the river floods every spring. A weak bridge would be closed for weeks. So the stronger design was selected. The extra cost was about two million. That money came from the regional budget. Now, a trade-off means you gain one thing and lose another. Here, the town gained safety but lost some parkland. Three old trees were removed. Some residents were unhappy. They said the trees were important. The council agreed to plant ten new trees nearby. Those trees will grow slowly. So the benefit is delayed.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "042d2e7d1e3fe15be95c936c777ce5c309d0c88cc7b9b45b8e33ac252d6f9399",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "MAYA",
        "sourceStart": 784,
        "sourceEnd": 820,
        "text": "Excuse me, was the public consulted?",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "2ceff16521f38f254cf0a6492ee76b0fdd1b05b9a9513652063146b2f69b066f",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "lecturer",
        "speaker": "LECTURER",
        "sourceStart": 831,
        "sourceEnd": 887,
        "text": "Yes, two meetings were held. But only forty people came.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "e64fc32a341b22b951576b6bfdc97726e13301072b1f1631bb0b07426dbd53da",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "maya",
        "speaker": "MAYA",
        "sourceStart": 894,
        "sourceEnd": 924,
        "text": "So most people were not heard.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-EricNeural",
        "textHash": "fa59956ef1432eebb3b9131efa7f6644f14e5019d40f6b7ac60259c7b89607f5",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      },
      {
        "role": "lecturer",
        "speaker": "LECTURER",
        "sourceStart": 935,
        "sourceEnd": 2094,
        "text": "That is a fair point. The process was criticized for that reason. Now, look at the timeline. The design was finished in March. Construction started in June. The bridge was opened in December. It was used by 800 people on the first day. That number was higher than expected. The old ferry was closed afterwards. Some ferry workers were retrained. Others were let go. This is another trade-off. Jobs were lost, but travel time was cut. Before, the crossing took twenty minutes. Now it takes four minutes. The saved time helps local shops. Deliveries are faster. However, the bridge is not perfect. It was built for walkers and cyclists only. Cars cannot use it. So drivers still use the old road bridge. That bridge is often crowded. A wider road bridge was proposed, but it was rejected. The cost was too high. So the problem was only partly solved. Let me finish with a question. When you read about infrastructure, ask: who gains, who loses, and who decides? These three questions are useful. They are not always easy to answer. But they help you read critically. Next week we will look at a water project. Please read the short case before class. Thank you.",
        "boundary": "turn",
        "voice": "en-US-JennyNeural",
        "textHash": "da5014cd39e1c6d48871b2a2c3320dce6c3fcee9a84a5c31c00975b4e4edf543",
        "pauseBeforeSeconds": 0.45
      }
    ]
  },
